WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.840
A SOC 2 Type II report evaluates both control design and operating effectiveness over a

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.820
defined review period.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.080
The review period may vary, but the key idea is consistent operation over time.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.860
A Type II report asks not only whether the control was designed suitably,

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:23.160
but whether it actually operated as described during the period.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:26.660
This is why Type II usually requires more evidence discipline.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.360
A control owner cannot rely on one screenshot from the report date if the control is

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:32.780
supposed to

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.800
run weekly, monthly, quarterly, or whenever a triggering event occurs.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:42.340
The evidence needs to show that the control operated at the required frequency,

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.560
that exceptions were identified,

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:50.380
and that remediation or follow-up happened when needed. Consider access reviews.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:55.560
For Type II, the team may need evidence that reviews occurred during the review period,

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:00.340
that the right population was reviewed, that reviewers had appropriate responsibility,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.320
that exceptions were tracked,

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.260
and that access changes were completed.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:10.840
For change management, evidence may include tickets, approvals, testing notes,

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:14.100
deployment records, and emergency change follow-up.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:20.080
For monitoring, evidence may include alert configurations, review records, escalations,

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.740
and incident handling.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:28.120
Type II also tests whether the organization can sustain the program when the audit is not

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:29.380
the only

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.600
thing happening. People join and leave. Services change. Vendors are added. Incidents

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.060
occur. Systems are patched.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.320
Controls need to operate through normal business conditions.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:46.900
That is why Type II often provides stronger assurance to customers: it shows operation,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.320
not only design.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:53.420
Operating effectiveness does not mean there can never be an exception. Real programs have

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:54.680
exceptions.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:00.020
The important question is whether exceptions are identified, documented, evaluated,

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.720
remediated, and explained honestly.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.740
Trying to hide control failures or backfill evidence dishonestly undermines the entire

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.360
purpose of SOC 2.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:14.420
A sustainable program treats exceptions as signals for improvement.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.160
For teams preparing for Type II,

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:20.140
the best approach is to build evidence collection into normal workflows.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:25.000
Ticketing, access review tools, HR processes, monitoring systems,

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:29.460
incident response records, backup tests, vendor review workflows,

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.120
and policy acknowledgments should produce evidence as work happens.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:37.260
If evidence collection depends on a frantic scramble at the end of the review period,

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.120
the program is harder to trust and harder to operate.
